# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env* is gitignored except this file.
#
# THERE ARE NO AWS CREDENTIALS HERE, and there never should be. Both hosts get
# their DynamoDB permissions from an EC2 instance role, which the AWS SDK finds
# on its own. If you are pasting an AKIA... key anywhere, the deployment is
# wrong -- see docs/aws-setup.md.

# --- Discord OAuth -----------------------------------------------------------
# https://discord.com/developers/applications -> your app -> OAuth2
# The client id is public by nature (it travels in the authorize URL).
DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_ME
DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_ME

# Right-click your Discord server -> Copy Server ID (needs Developer Mode on).
# Membership here is a coarse filter before any permission check runs.
DISCORD_GUILD_ID=REPLACE_ME

# The Discord role required to sign in at all. Server Settings -> Roles ->
# right-click the role -> Copy Role ID. Coarser than the grants table and
# checked first: guild membership stopped meaning anything once the guild is
# also the player community.
#
# IT IS ALSO RE-CHECKED BEFORE EVERY WRITE, not only at the door -- see
# DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN below and lib/discordRole.ts.
DISCORD_ADMIN_ROLE_ID=REPLACE_ME

# OPTIONAL, and it now does two jobs. Discord Developer Portal -> your app ->
# Bot -> Reset Token. ONE TOKEN, NOT TWO: a second bot credential would be a
# second thing to rotate and a second thing to leak.
#
# (1) REAL PROFILE PICTURES. A Discord user id cannot be turned into an avatar
# URL on its own: the CDN path needs the account's current avatar hash, and only
# the API knows it. Without this, every player shows one of Discord's six
# generic default logos -- which is a picture, but never that person's.
#
# ONE UNCACHED CALL PER PROFILE PAGE VIEW, with a five-second ceiling. A profile
# page is opened a few dozen times a day, which is nowhere near the rate limit,
# and a cached avatar is simply the wrong avatar. The page renders without it
# rather than waiting -- a slow Discord costs the picture and nothing else.
#
# (2) THE ADMIN-ROLE RE-CHECK BEFORE EVERY WRITE. Ringmaster's permissions live
# in DynamoDB and are independent of Discord, so somebody kicked from your
# Discord server -- or merely stripped of the admin role there -- kept a working
# console until a human revoked their grants row by hand. Before every action
# that changes something (ban, lift, kick, incident closure, maintenance, branch
# switch, deploy) the console asks Discord whether that account still holds
# DISCORD_ADMIN_ROLE_ID in DISCORD_GUILD_ID. If Discord says no, the write is
# refused, the session is deleted, and the admin is told exactly why.
#
# ONE UNCACHED CALL PER WRITE, waiting up to five seconds. Writes are bans,
# kicks and deploys -- tens per day against a documented ceiling of fifty
# requests per second -- so there is nothing a cache would save, and a cached
# "still an admin" is the one answer this check exists to avoid.
#
# IF DISCORD DOES NOT ANSWER IN FIVE SECONDS THE WRITE GOES AHEAD, loudly, with
# a row in the audit log saying the check did not resolve. The DynamoDB grant is
# the primary permission and it has already been checked live on that same
# request; failing closed would take every moderation tool offline during a
# Discord outage, which is exactly when moderation is most needed.
#
# JOB (2) CHANGED WHAT THE BOT NEEDS. Read this twice if you set the token up
# before this feature existed: job (1) works from outside your server, but job
# (2) requires THE BOT TO BE A MEMBER OF THE GUILD in DISCORD_GUILD_ID. Invite
# it with no permissions at all -- it needs none. It still needs NO PRIVILEGED
# INTENTS (only listing all members requires one; fetching a single member does
# not), still cannot read messages, and still cannot act on anyone.
#
# Leave it unset and the console still works: profile pictures fall back to the
# generic logos and the role re-check is disabled, with a warning logged on
# every write. See lib/discord.ts and lib/discordRole.ts.
DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=

# --- Auth.js -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET=REPLACE_ME_WITH_32_PLUS_RANDOM_CHARS

# Public origin. Must match the redirect URI registered in Discord, which is
# this value + /api/auth/callback/discord
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- AWS ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Region of the DynamoDB tables -- co-located with the game server, not with
# Ringmaster. See docs/aws-setup.md.
AWS_REGION=us-east-2
DDB_TABLE_PREFIX=ringmaster-

# --- Ingest ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret the game server presents when pushing realtime state. The
# endpoint is only reachable over the VPC peering link, so this is defence in
# depth -- but the primary control is a security group, and security groups get
# edited by tired people.
#   openssl rand -hex 24
INGEST_SECRET=REPLACE_ME

# --- Command credential -------------------------------------------------------
# The shared secret blitz-bot presents, in x-ringmaster-service, so that
# /brkick, /brban and /drain can act through the console instead of around it.
#
# NAMED FOR THE ENDPOINT FAMILY IT GUARDS, the way INGEST_SECRET is -- the kick,
# ban and drain command routes. Unprefixed on purpose, because the SAME value
# goes in this file and in the bot's own .env: it belongs to the pair, like
# DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN, and unlike the bot's own BLITZ_* settings.
#
# A SECOND SECRET, NOT INGEST_SECRET: that one lives on the game box, and a
# game box compromise must not also be able to ban players and restart servers.
#
# OPTIONAL. Left empty, the bot path is closed and every command call is
# refused with a line in the journal naming this variable. Nothing else changes.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
COMMAND_SECRET=

# --- Game host SSH (Host page) -----------------------------------------------
# The forced-command channel to the game box. All optional -- unset, the Host
# page shows "not configured". The private key never leaves this box.
#   GAME_HOST      the game box private IP over the peering link
#   GAME_SSH_KEY   path to the private key, e.g. /opt/ringmaster/.ssh/dispatch
GAME_HOST=REPLACE_ME
GAME_SSH_USER=ubuntu
GAME_SSH_KEY=/opt/ringmaster/.ssh/dispatch
